e Royal Palaces \n Share \n Language \r\n På svenska \r\n In English \r\n Suomeksi \r\n Auf Deutsch \r\n Français \r\n En español \r\n In italiano \r\n Русский \r\n 日本語 \r\n 中文 \r\n باللغة العربية \r\n Activate listen \n SV \n Enter search terms \n Search \n Search \n Menu \n The Chinese Pavilion \n Startsida \n English \n Royal palaces and sites \n The Chinese Pavilion \n Share \n Language \n På svenska \n In English \n Suomeksi \n Auf Deutsch \n Français \n En español \n In italiano \n Русский \n 日本語 \n 中文 \n باللغة العربية \n Activate listen\n Samtycke för funktionella kakor\n För att använda lyssnafunktionen behöver du lämna ditt samtycke till funktionella kakor.\n Samtyck till funktionella kakor\n Avbryt\n The Chinese Pavilion\n “He took me to the side of the pleasure gardens, and I was surprised to find myself suddenly standing in front of a real fairy tale palace, as the King had commissioned a Chinese pavilion – the most beautiful building imaginable.”\n One July evening in 1753, Queen Lovisa Ulrika was surprised with a fantastic birthday present. In the far section of Drottningholm Palace Park, King Adolf Fredrik had secretly had a summer palace built in the Chinese style. At that time, all things Chinese were the latest fashion.\n The East India trading companies brought large quantities of tea, spices, silk, porcelain and exclusive works of art to Europe during the 18th century. China was seen as an exotic, mythical country, and the Chinese Pavilion is the embodiment of this oriental fantasy.\n Inside the pavilion, Chinese-inspired Swedish Rococo furniture stands alongside imported Chinese objects. Several of the rooms still have their original Chinese silk and paper wall coverings. There are also lacquered screens, stained glass, porcelain and other decorative objects, many of which were probably imported by the Swedish East India Company. However, some of the Chinese objects here are even older, including pieces from the times of Queen Hedvig Eleonora and Queen Kristina, when porcelain was incredibly expensive.\n The Chinese Pavilion, together with Drottningholm Palace and its grounds, is on UNESCO&#39;s World Heritage List.\n Photo: Raphael Stecksén/Royalpalaces.se \n Tickets \n Opening hours \r\n Visit us \r\n Guided tour \n App with guides \n For children \n Group Visits \n Getting here \n Admission charges \n Practical information, Access \n Contact us \n Travel companies and authorised guides \n Discover more at The Chinese Pavilion \r\n The Pavilion \n Drottningholm Palace Park \n Pavilion Café \n Souvenirs \n History \n Visit us \n Visit us\n Guided tour \n The Chinese Pavilion is open during the summer months. Stroll on your own with the free...\n App with guides \n The Swedish Royal Palaces app includes a number of features to enhance your visit to th...\n For children \n Go on a journey of discovery through the rooms of the Chinese Pavilion! The picture hun...\n Discover more at The Chinese Pavilion\n The rooms of the Chinese Pavilion \n The Chinese Pavilion was built in the middle of the 1700s, a period in European history...\n Drottningholm Palace Park \n Drottningholm Palace Park is open all year round. Here, you can wander through historic...\n The Chinese Pavilion C afé \n In Drottningholm Palace Park, close to the Chinese Pavilion, you will find the Pavilion...\n Souvenirs from the Chinese Pavilion \n The Royal Gift Shop is a unique present and souvenir shop offering products with a conn...\n History \n The first pavilion, a prefabricated building, was erected here in 1753 as a birthday pr...\n Visit us , The Chinese Pavilion \n Articles and movies\n Autumn at the Royal palaces \n Explore three royal sites with self-guided visits and guided tours. You can also step i...\n Late summer at the Royal Palaces \n Late summer invites to seven open Royal sites, a historical retrospective at the Royal ...\n Royal Walks \n The Royal Walks app will guide you through the royal parks – either on site or from ho